# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kasuwa local development environment.
# Copy to .env — `pnpm dev` does this for you if .env is missing.
# Never put real secrets in this file; it is committed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# NODE_ENV is deliberately NOT set here.
#
# Nx injects this file into every task it runs, so setting NODE_ENV=development
# leaks into `next build` and breaks it in a way that does not reproduce when
# the same script is run directly. The API and the worker refuse to start with
# NODE_ENV unset (D-L95, T-0117); `pnpm dev` (tools/dev.mjs) sets
# NODE_ENV=development per process, so nothing needs it here.
LOG_LEVEL=debug

# --- Auth -------------------------------------------------------------------
# `stub` resolves the caller from x-kasuwa-* request headers (dev/test only).
# The API refuses to boot with KASUWA_AUTH_MODE=stub when NODE_ENV=production.
# See docs/adr/0007-stubbed-auth-context.md and team/DECISIONS.md D-L05.
KASUWA_AUTH_MODE=stub

# --- Envelope encryption (D-L13) --------------------------------------------
# TOTP secrets are stored encrypted: a data key per secret, wrapped by this
# environment's KMS key. `local` is the DEVELOPMENT provider — it keeps its
# master key in the application process, which protects a stolen backup and
# nothing else. The API refuses to boot with it when NODE_ENV=production, and
# the provider refuses to be constructed there at all.
#
# Left unset, the local provider uses a published development master key, which
# is what lets `pnpm db:seed` and the API agree with no configuration. There is
# nothing to protect locally: the seeded secrets are committed in
# packages/db/src/fixtures.ts.
#
# Production: KASUWA_KMS_PROVIDER=aws-kms and KASUWA_KMS_KEY_ID=<arn or alias>.
# Nothing is provisioned yet (D-A4), so that combination currently fails at
# boot with a configuration error rather than at the first sign-in.
KASUWA_KMS_PROVIDER=local
# KASUWA_KMS_KEY_ID=local-dev-1
# KASUWA_KMS_LOCAL_MASTER_KEY=   # base64, 32 bytes. Optional, development only.

# --- Postgres ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Note the non-default host port: the dev stack avoids 5432/6379/9200 so it
# cannot collide with, or be shadowed by, a natively installed service.
# kasuwa_app has NO BYPASSRLS and no DDL rights. The API, worker and realtime
# processes use it and nothing else.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://kasuwa_app:kasuwa_app_dev@localhost:55432/kasuwa
# kasuwa_auth has NO BYPASSRLS and belongs to no role. It is the only role
# that may write the auth tables (sessions, challenges, credentials, MFA
# factors, backup codes), and only the API's auth module uses it (T-0049,
# D-P16). The API refuses to boot without it; the web process refuses to
# start WITH it, as with DATABASE_URL.
DATABASE_AUTH_URL=postgres://kasuwa_auth:kasuwa_auth_dev@localhost:55432/kasuwa
# kasuwa_relay (the worker's outbox relay, ADR-0018) is deliberately NOT set
# in this file: every server process loads .env, and the API, realtime and
# the operator commands refuse that connection by presence. `pnpm dev` hands
# it to the worker alone. Never add it here.
# kasuwa_migrator owns the schema and is used ONLY by the migration runner and
# the seed script.
DATABASE_MIGRATION_URL=postgres://kasuwa_migrator:kasuwa_migrator_dev@localhost:55432/kasuwa

# --- Redis ------------------------------------------------------------------
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:56379

# --- OpenSearch -------------------------------------------------------------
# Present for local parity only; nothing in the scaffold queries it yet.
OPENSEARCH_URL=http://localhost:59200

# --- Object storage (SeaweedFS, not MinIO - see D-L21) ----------------------
# Read through storageEnv() in @kasuwa/config (T-0092, ADR-0017). The dev store
# is CREDENTIALLED: the two keys below are the development identity in
# tools/seaweedfs/s3-identities.json, invented and committed on purpose. They
# work only against the compose SeaweedFS and must never be used anywhere real.
#
# S3_ENDPOINT is the host the API and worker call; S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT is the
# host signed into the browser's presigned upload URLs. They are the same here
# because the apps run on the host; they differ when the API runs inside the
# Docker network. The web process may hold S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT only, never
# S3_ENDPOINT or the keys (T-0014, T-0092 R9).
#
# S3_SSE_MODE: AES256 (SSE-S3, the default) or off. `off` is refused outside
# development and test (D-L94, D-L128 Q3).
S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:59000
S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:59000
S3_REGION=af-south-1
S3_BUCKET=kasuwa-local
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true
S3_SSE_MODE=AES256
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=kasuwa-dev-access
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=kasuwa-dev-secret-not-a-real-key

# --- Apps -------------------------------------------------------------------
API_PORT=3001
WEB_PORT=3000
REALTIME_PORT=3002
# The web process loads no .env (T-0014): tools/dev.mjs hands it these two,
# the browser session layer's settings (ADR-0019, T-0125). Listed here so the
# names are discoverable. KASUWA_WEB_ORIGIN is the web app's own origin as the
# browser sends it (open the app at exactly this address, or every write is
# refused as CSRF); KASUWA_API_INTERNAL_URL is where its BFF reaches the API,
# server-side. There is no NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL: the browser never needs the
# API's address. For a browser that will not keep a Secure cookie on
# http://localhost (some Safari builds), start with
# KASUWA_SESSION_COOKIE_INSECURE=1 in the shell; never anywhere but here.
KASUWA_WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000
KASUWA_API_INTERNAL_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3001
# The web tier is NOT a proxy hop (D-L181 N3): the BFF forwards X-Forwarded-For
# untouched, so the API's KASUWA_TRUST_PROXY_HOPS counts only the proxies in
# front of the web server. Never expose the web server directly while the API
# trusts 1 or more hops: the client would choose its own sign-in throttle key.

# --- Observability (T-0005) -------------------------------------------------
# `cell` on every API span. `console` prints spans and logs to stdout; both
# carry allow-listed attributes only (apps/api/src/telemetry/attributes.ts).
KASUWA_CELL=local
KASUWA_OTEL_EXPORTER=none

# --- i18n -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing here. @kasuwa/i18n derives the offered languages from catalogue
# completeness plus a named reviewer (D-P1, D-L27). An environment variable
# that could widen that set would route around the gate silently.
